---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import ResultsTable from '../../components/ResultsTable.astro';
import Fig from '../../components/Fig.astro';
import Tex from '../../components/Tex.astro';
import PermutationTree from '../../components/figures/PermutationTree.astro';
import walk from '../../assets/figures/mhv-walk.png';
import terminal from '../../assets/figures/mhv-terminal.png';
import five from '../../assets/figures/mhv-five.png';

const src = 'Verma & Chandrashekar, arXiv:2607.02456.';
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="mhv-quantum-walks"
  setting="Quantum Optics & Quantum Information Laboratory, Department of Electronic Systems Engineering, IISc Bengaluru"
  contribution={[{ text: 'The research in this paper is my own work.', basis: 'Author’s statement, October 2026.' }]}
  collaborators={[
    {
      text: 'C. M. Chandrashekar is the second author and supervised the project.',
      basis: 'Author list of arXiv:2607.02456; supervisor of my Project Assistant position (CV).',
    },
  ]}
>
  <Fragment slot="problem">
    <p>
      In perturbative quantum chromodynamics, an amplitude with many external gluons splits into color factors and
      color-ordered partial amplitudes, one for each ordering of the gluons. The number of orderings grows factorially
      with the number of gluons, even though, for maximally helicity violating (MHV) configurations, each partial
      amplitude has the compact Parke–Taylor form.
    </p>
    <p>
      The question here is whether that combinatorial structure can be given a dynamical representation, with the
      orderings explored by a quantum walk, as a step towards quantum algorithms that simulate scattering processes.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="method">
    <p>The full amplitude is a sum over color orderings σ, and for MHV gluons each ordered amplitude is a ratio of spinor products:</p>
    <Tex block tex={String.raw`\mathcal{M}_n=\sum_{\sigma} C(\sigma)\,A(\sigma),\qquad A(1^-,2^+,\dots,k^-,\dots,n^+)=\frac{\langle 1k\rangle^4}{\langle 12\rangle\langle 23\rangle\cdots\langle n1\rangle}`} />
    <p>The construction has four parts:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        A directed permutation tree. Each node is a partial ordering <Tex tex={String.raw`(1,a_2,\dots,a_m)`} />, each edge appends one
        remaining gluon, and every root-to-terminal path is one complete color ordering.
      </li>
      <li>
        A coined quantum walk with evolution <Tex tex="U = SC" /> on that tree. Local coin operators assign transition amplitudes from the
        spinor-product structure, so the amplitude accumulated along a path carries that ordering’s Parke–Taylor denominator.
      </li>
      <li>
        A quantum channel built from Kraus operators that extracts sector-resolved quantities proportional to{' '}
        <Tex tex={String.raw`|A|^2`} />, and a weighted collection operator that coherently maps all terminal sectors to one reference node.
      </li>
      <li>A quantum Fourier transform on the coin space that combines the encoded contributions into the color-decomposed amplitude.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The paper then checks the construction numerically for low-point gluon amplitudes against analytical results.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="results">
    <p>
      The paper reports that the walk reproduces the Parke–Taylor structure and that the reconstructed low-point
      amplitudes are <Claim id="mhv.numerics" />, with the comparison given in its Table II. The tree has{' '}
      <Claim id="mhv.sectors" /> terminal sectors, and its size and the walk’s Hilbert-space dimension grow as listed in Table VI.
    </p>
    <ResultsTable ids={['mhv.numerics', 'mhv.sectors', 'mhv.growth']} />
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="interactive">
    <p>Switch between four, five and six gluons, move the walk step, and select a leaf to trace its color ordering.</p>
    <PermutationTree />
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="figures">
    <Fig src={walk} alt="Four bar charts of walker probability over tree nodes at steps t = 0 to 3." caption="Fig. 3. Probability distribution of the quantum walker after successive applications of the evolution operator U = SC. The final step corresponds to the terminal permutation sectors." source={src} />
    <Fig src={terminal} alt="Bar chart of occupation probabilities across the six terminal permutation sectors." caption="Fig. 4. Occupation probabilities of the terminal permutation sectors after completion of the quantum walk." source={src} />
    <Fig src={five} alt="Radial permutation graph for five gluons with 24 labelled terminal orderings." caption="Fig. 9. Permutation graph for the five-gluon scattering process." source={src} />
  </Fragment>
</CaseStudy>
